(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-nephrology-urinary-quiz"]={"title":"Nephrology / Urinary System — Anatomy Exam 3","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Which four organs make up the urinary system?","choices":["Kidneys, ureters, prostate, and urethra","Kidneys, adrenal glands, ureters, and bladder","Kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra","Nephrons, ureters, bladder, and rectum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The urinary system consists of the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"The primary organs of the urinary system that filter waste and form urine are the:","choices":["Ureters","Urethra","Bladder","Kidneys"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidneys are the primary organs; they filter waste from the bloodstream and convert filtrate into urine. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"The medical term for urination is:","choices":["Micturition","Defecation","Deglutition","Mastication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Micturition is the medical term for urination; the ureters, bladder, and urethra transport urine out of the body. — Slide 4 (Systems Involved)"},{"q":"The ureters, bladder, and urethra together form the:","choices":["Urinary tract (for transport of urine)","Nephron, the microscopic filtering unit","Renal corpuscle, glomerulus plus capsule","Juxtaglomerular apparatus cluster"],"answer":0,"exp":"The urinary tract (ureters, bladder, urethra) transports urine out of the body after the kidneys produce it. — Slide 4 (Systems Involved)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the kidneys are:","choices":["Intraperitoneal","Suspended by a mesentery","Subperitoneal","Retroperitoneal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidneys are retroperitoneal organs; the right sits about T12–L3 and the left about T10/11–L2. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The concave medial border of the kidney, where vessels and nerves enter, is the:","choices":["Hilum","Renal sinus","Renal papilla","Renal pelvis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hilum is the concave medial border through which the nerves, arteries, and veins (NAVs) enter and the renal pelvis exits. — Slides 6, 10 (Kidney anatomy)"},{"q":"Listing from deep (nearest kidney) to superficial, the tissue layers surrounding the kidney are:","choices":["Renal fascia → fibrous capsule → perinephric fat → paranephric fat","Paranephric fat → renal fascia → perinephric fat → fibrous capsule","Fibrous capsule → perinephric fat → renal fascia → paranephric fat","Perinephric fat → fibrous capsule → paranephric fat → renal fascia"],"answer":2,"exp":"From deep to superficial: fibrous (renal) capsule, perinephric (adipose) fat, renal fascia, then paranephric fat, then peritoneum. — Slides 11, 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The renal medulla is divided into cone-shaped structures whose apices (renal papillae) point toward the renal sinus; these are the:","choices":["Renal columns","Renal pyramids","Renal calyces","Renal corpuscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medulla is divided into 8–15 renal pyramids per kidney; the base abuts the cortex and the apex is the renal papilla. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The cortical tissue that dips between the renal pyramids is called the:","choices":["Renal papilla","Minor calyx","Renal sinus","Renal columns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Renal columns are extensions of cortex between the pyramids. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The internal space of the kidney that houses the vessels, renal pelvis, and calyces (plus fat) is the:","choices":["Renal cortex","Renal sinus","Renal papilla","Bowman's capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"The renal sinus is the internal space housing blood/lymphatic vessels, nerves, renal pelvis, and calyces, embedded in fat. — Slide 10 (Gross and Sectional Anatomy)"},{"q":"The dense irregular connective tissue layer that directly covers the outer surface (cortex) of the kidney is the:","choices":["Fibrous (renal) capsule","Renal fascia layer","Paranephric fat layer","Peritoneum surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fibrous (renal) capsule is dense irregular connective tissue covering the cortex; the renal fascia anchors the kidney to the posterior wall. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly traces arterial blood from the aorta to the glomerulus?","choices":["Renal artery → afferent arteriole → arcuate → interlobar → glomerulus directly","Renal artery → arcuate → interlobar → segmental → afferent arteriole → glomerulus","Renal artery → interlobular → arcuate → interlobar → efferent arteriole → glomerulus","Renal artery → segmental → interlobar → arcuate → interlobular → afferent arteriole → glomerulus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Aorta → renal → segmental → lobar → interlobar → arcuate → interlobular → afferent arteriole → glomerulus. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Blood leaves the glomerulus via the efferent arteriole, which then branches into peritubular capillaries or the:","choices":["Interlobar vein","Arcuate artery","Segmental artery","Vasa recta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Efferent arterioles form peritubular capillaries (juxtaposed to the nephron) or the vasa recta, where solute exchange occurs. — Slide 18 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"The renal vein drains into which large vessel?","choices":["Portal vein","Azygos vein","Inferior vena cava","Superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal vein drains to the inferior vena cava; on the left it also receives the gonadal vein. — Slides 6, 18 (renal venous drainage)"},{"q":"Which sequence traces venous blood correctly from the vasa recta back toward the IVC?","choices":["Vasa recta → arcuate vein → interlobular vein → segmental vein → IVC","Vasa recta → renal vein → arcuate vein → interlobar vein → IVC","Vasa recta → interlobular vein → arcuate vein → interlobar vein → renal vein → IVC","Vasa recta → interlobar vein → interlobular vein → renal vein → IVC"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vasa recta → interlobular vein → arcuate vein → interlobar vein → renal vein → inferior vena cava. — Slide 18 (Blood Supply, venous)"},{"q":"Blood plasma is filtered across which structure into the glomerular (capsular) space?","choices":["The efferent arteriole","The collecting duct","The vasa recta","The glomerulus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Plasma is filtered across the glomerulus into the glomerular space of Bowman's capsule. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"On the left side, the renal vein additionally receives which vein before draining to the IVC?","choices":["The left gonadal vein","The right gonadal vein","The azygos vein","The splenic vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left renal vein receives the left gonadal (and left suprarenal) vein before entering the IVC. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerulus and the:","choices":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle segment","Glomerular (Bowman's) capsule","Collecting duct segment"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal corpuscle = glomerulus + glomerular (Bowman's) capsule; it is the filtering unit of the nephron. — Slide 29 (Nephron Structures)"},{"q":"Which segment of the renal tubule begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle and is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium?","choices":["Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct system","Ascending limb of Henle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The proximal convoluted tubule begins at the corpuscle's tubular pole and is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium. — Slide 38 (Proximal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"Which type of nephron has long loops of Henle that extend deep into the medulla and possess vasa recta to concentrate urine?","choices":["Cortical nephrons near the periphery","Juxtamedullary (medullary) nephrons","Aglomerular nephrons lacking a glomerulus","Superficial nephrons near the cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%) have long loops and vasa recta to recover water/Na+/Cl- and concentrate urine; cortical nephrons (~85%) have short loops and peritubular capillaries. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of nephrons are cortical (short-looped) nephrons producing 'standard' urine?","choices":["85%","50%","15%","5%"],"answer":0,"exp":"About 85% of nephrons are cortical type; ~15% are juxtamedullary. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly traces filtrate through the nephron tubules?","choices":["Collecting duct → DCT → loop of Henle → PCT → glomerulus, reversed order","DCT → PCT → loop of Henle → collecting duct → papillary duct","PCT → descending limb → loop of Henle → ascending limb → DCT → collecting duct","PCT → DCT → descending limb → ascending limb → collecting duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Filtrate flows: Bowman's → PCT → descending limb → loop of Henle → ascending limb → DCT → collecting duct → papillary duct. — Slides 22, 42 (Flow of Urine)"},{"q":"The distal convoluted tubule begins at the end of the thick ascending limb and lies adjacent to the afferent arteriole, forming part of the:","choices":["Renal corpuscle itself","Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renal sinus cavity","Vasa recta network"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DCT lies next to the afferent arteriole, contributing to the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — Slide 40 (Distal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The loop of Henle has two limbs; the descending limb runs from the cortex/PCT toward the medulla, and the ascending limb:","choices":["Returns to the renal cortex and leads into the DCT","Continues descending deeper into the medulla","Empties directly into a minor calyx","Becomes the collecting duct directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ascending limb (thin then thick) returns toward the cortex and leads into the distal convoluted tubule. — Slide 39 (Loop of Henle)"},{"q":"Once filtrate enters the collecting ducts, it:","choices":["Can no longer be modified and officially becomes urine","Undergoes extensive further modification still","Returns retrograde back to the glomerulus","Is reabsorbed into the vasa recta entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"No further modification occurs once filtrate is in the collecting ducts; it is now officially urine. Multiple collecting ducts converge at a renal papilla. — Slide 41 (Collecting Tubule & Ducts)"},{"q":"The ureters are retroperitoneal fibromuscular tubes about how long, conducting urine from the renal pelvis to the bladder?","choices":["About 5 cm","About 50 cm","About 25 cm","About 3 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ureters average ~25 cm, are retroperitoneal, originate at the renal pelvis, and enter the posterolateral bladder base. — Slide 44 (Ureters)"},{"q":"In the 'water under the bridge' relationship, the ureter passes deep/inferior to which vessels bilaterally?","choices":["The renal artery and vein at the hilum","The common iliac artery, near the pelvic brim","The testicular or ovarian (gonadal) artery and vein","The inferior vena cava, lying medially"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ureter travels deep (inferior) to the gonadal (testicular/ovarian) vessels — 'water (ureter) under the bridge (gonadal vessels)'. — Slide 48 ('Water under the bridge')"},{"q":"The urinary bladder lies immediately superior and posterior to which structure?","choices":["The kidneys above","The sacrum behind","The umbilicus above","The pubic symphysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bladder lies immediately superior and posterior to the pubic symphysis. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The bladder is a non-peritoneal organ EXCEPT for which wall, which is covered by peritoneum?","choices":["The inferior wall","The anterior wall","The superior wall","The posterior wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Only the superior wall of the bladder is peritoneal; this affects where bladder pain is sensed. — Slides 49, 53 (Bladder)"},{"q":"In females, the organ located posterosuperior to the bladder is the:","choices":["Rectum","Prostate","Uterus","Sigmoid colon"],"answer":2,"exp":"In females, the uterus is posterosuperior and the vagina posteroinferior to the bladder; in males the rectum is posterosuperior and the prostate inferior. — Slide 51 (Urinary Bladder relationships)"},{"q":"The median umbilical ligament, related to the bladder, is a remnant of which fetal structure?","choices":["The gubernaculum","The urachus","The mesonephric duct","The ductus venosus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The median umbilical ligament is the remnant of the urachus, the fetal tube that returned urine to the mother via the umbilical cord. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The blood supply to the bladder runs: aorta → common iliac → internal iliac → anterior division → superior and:","choices":["Inferior mesenteric artery","Inferior vesical arteries","Gonadal arteries","Middle rectal arteries only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The bladder is supplied by the superior and inferior vesical arteries from the anterior division of the internal iliac; venous drainage is via the vesical venous plexus to the internal iliac veins. — Slide 56 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Approximate urethral length differs by sex: the female urethra is about 3–4 cm, whereas the male urethra is about:","choices":["8–10 cm","2 cm","25 cm","18–20 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The female urethra is ~3–4 cm; the male urethra is ~18–20 cm and has prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) regions. — Slides 58–59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"Common sites where kidney stones lodge in the ureter include the ureteropelvic junction, where the ureters cross the common iliac artery, and the:","choices":["Renal papilla tip","Urethral meatus","Ureterovesical junction","Renal sinus cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stones commonly lodge at the UPJ, at the common iliac crossing, and at the ureterovesical junction — the three natural constrictions. — Slide 47 (Zones Related to the Ureters)"},{"q":"The membranous portion of the male urethra is distinguished anatomically because it pierces the:","choices":["Urogenital diaphragm","Prostate gland itself","Corpus spongiosum","Renal fascia layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"The membranous urethra runs from the prostatic urethra to the penile urethra and pierces the urogenital diaphragm. — Slide 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The lining of the ureters, which gives them stretch, is composed of which epithelium?","choices":["Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ureteral mucosa is transitional epithelium, allowing distension; the muscularis has inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle (plus an external longitudinal layer distally). — Slide 45 (Ureter Walls)"},{"q":"The inner lining of the urinary bladder is which stretchy epithelium?","choices":["Simple columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The bladder's inner lining is transitional (stretchy) epithelium; it can expand ~15× its empty volume to hold ~500 mL. — Slide 53 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The parietal layer of Bowman's capsule is composed of which epithelium?","choices":["Simple squamous epithelium","Podocytes","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The parietal (outer) layer of Bowman's capsule is simple squamous epithelium; the visceral layer is made of podocytes. — Slide 35 (Renal Corpuscle)"},{"q":"The visceral layer of the glomerular capsule is formed by specialized cells with foot processes (pedicles) called:","choices":["Chromaffin cells","Mesangial cells","Chief cells","Podocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Podocytes form the visceral layer; their pedicles create filtration slits and are coated in negatively charged glycocalyx. — Slides 33–35 (Glomerulus / Renal Corpuscle)"},{"q":"The glomerular capillary endothelium is described as ______, allowing high permeability during filtration.","choices":["Continuous","Sinusoidal only","Fenestrated","Stratified"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glomerular endothelium is fenestrated (large pores), making it highly permeable; a negatively charged basement membrane and podocyte slits restrict large anions/proteins. — Slides 33–34 (Filtration Membranes)"},{"q":"The detrusor muscle of the bladder wall is composed of which muscle type arranged in three layers?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The detrusor is smooth muscle (inner and outer longitudinal around a middle circular layer), forming the muscular middle layer of the bladder. — Slide 53 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The macula densa, a sensor of tubular NaCl concentration within the juxtaglomerular apparatus, is a cluster of specialized cells located in the:","choices":["Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle descending limb"],"answer":0,"exp":"The macula densa is in the distal (convoluted) tubule and senses NaCl to help regulate GFR and renin release. — Slides 36, 40 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus / DCT)"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells, modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole, synthesize, store, and secrete:","choices":["Erythropoietin","Renin","Calcitriol","Aldosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells secrete renin in response to decreased blood pressure or macula densa signals; EPO-producing/oxygen-sensing (REPOS) cells secrete erythropoietin. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"},{"q":"Which organs make up the urinary tract that transports urine out of the body, as distinct from the kidneys that form it?","choices":["Ureters, urinary bladder, urethra","Ureters, spleen, and urethra together","Renal pelvis, liver, urethra","Ureters, adrenal glands, bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The urinary tract — ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra — transports urine out of the body once the kidneys have formed it. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"After the kidneys produce urine, which structure conducts it from each kidney to the bladder?","choices":["Urethra","Renal pelvis","Collecting duct","Ureter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each ureter carries urine from the renal pelvis of the kidney to the bladder; the urethra then carries urine from the bladder out of the body. — Slide 4 (Systems Involved)"},{"q":"Which kidney typically sits slightly lower than the other because of the liver?","choices":["The left kidney","The right kidney","Both sit at exactly the same level","Neither — position varies randomly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right kidney (about T12–L3) sits slightly lower than the left (about T10/11–L2) because the liver occupies space superiorly on the right. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The funnel-shaped structure formed by the convergence of the major calyces, which collects urine before the ureter, is the:","choices":["Renal sinus","Renal papilla","Renal pelvis","Renal column"],"answer":2,"exp":"Minor calyces merge into major calyces, which merge into the renal pelvis; the pelvis funnels urine into the ureter. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"About what fraction of resting cardiac output do the kidneys receive?","choices":["About 5% only","About 75%","About 50%","About 20–25%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidneys receive roughly 20–25% of resting cardiac output, reflecting their high filtration workload. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Which vessel drains into the left renal vein before reaching the inferior vena cava, but drains directly into the IVC on the right side?","choices":["The gonadal vein","The hepatic vein","The splenic vein","The superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"On the left side, the gonadal vein empties into the renal vein before it reaches the IVC; on the right side, the gonadal vein drains directly into the IVC. — Nephrology/Urinary PPT — Kidney Location"},{"q":"Which is the correct order of nephron structures that filtrate passes through after the renal corpuscle?","choices":["PCT → DCT → loop of Henle → collecting duct","DCT → loop of Henle → PCT → collecting duct","Loop of Henle → PCT → DCT → collecting duct","PCT → loop of Henle → DCT → collecting duct"],"answer":3,"exp":"After the renal corpuscle, filtrate flows: proximal convoluted tubule → loop of Henle (descending then ascending) → distal convoluted tubule → collecting duct. — Slides 29, 42 (Nephron Structures / Flow of Urine)"},{"q":"Juxtamedullary nephrons, which make up ~15% of nephrons, are specialized for what function?","choices":["Producing 'standard,' unconcentrated urine","Concentrating urine via long loops of Henle and vasa recta","Secreting erythropoietin in response to hypoxia","Filtering large plasma proteins out of the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%) have long loops of Henle extending into the medulla and vasa recta, enabling recovery of water/Na+/Cl- and concentration of urine. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"Cortical nephrons, which make up about 85% of nephrons, are characterized by:","choices":["Short loops of Henle and peritubular capillaries","Long loops of Henle and vasa recta capillaries","No glomerulus present in the corpuscle","Location entirely within the medulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cortical nephrons (~85%) sit near the peripheral cortex, have short loops of Henle and peritubular capillaries, and produce 'standard' urine. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"The proximal convoluted tubule begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle and ends where which structure begins?","choices":["The distal convoluted tubule after the loop","The collecting duct after the DCT","The thick descending limb of the loop of Henle","The papillary duct at the pyramid tip"],"answer":2,"exp":"The PCT begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle and ends where the thick descending limb of the loop of Henle begins. — Slide 38 (Proximal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The distal convoluted tubule begins at the end of the thick ascending limb and lies adjacent to which structure, forming the juxtaglomerular apparatus?","choices":["The efferent arteriole","The afferent arteriole","The vasa recta","The collecting duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DCT begins at the end of the thick ascending limb and lies adjacent to the afferent arteriole, forming the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — Slide 40 (Distal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"Once filtrate enters the collecting ducts, what happens to its composition?","choices":["No further modifications can be made — it is officially urine","It undergoes major reabsorption of glucose there","It is returned upstream back to the glomerulus itself","It is filtered a second time at another site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Once in the collecting ducts the filtrate officially becomes urine; no further modifications are made. Multiple collecting ducts converge to form a renal papilla. — Slide 41 (Collecting Tubule & Ducts)"},{"q":"The kidneys are capped superiorly by which endocrine glands, which share the perinephric fat and renal fascia?","choices":["The thyroid glands, located in the neck","The gonads, located in the pelvis/scrotum","The parathyroid glands nearby","The adrenal (suprarenal) glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adrenal (suprarenal) glands sit atop each kidney, enclosed within the renal fascia and perinephric fat. — Slides 6, 11 (Kidney location and coverings)"},{"q":"Which posterior abdominal muscles form part of the bed on which the kidneys rest?","choices":["Rectus abdominis and the external oblique","Psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transversus abdominis","The diaphragm alone, without other muscles","Iliacus and sartorius of the thigh region"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidneys rest against the psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transversus abdominis of the posterior abdominal wall (with the diaphragm superiorly). — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The epithelium lining the proximal convoluted tubule, specialized for reabsorption with a prominent brush border, is:","choices":["Simple squamous epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The PCT is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium (with microvilli/brush border) suited to its heavy reabsorptive role. — Slide 38 (Proximal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The negatively charged coating on podocytes that helps repel anions and proteins during filtration is called:","choices":["Glycocalyx","Surfactant","Mucin","Keratin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Podocytes are coated in a negatively charged substance called glycocalyx, which (with the negatively charged basement membrane) helps prevent filtration of large anions and proteins such as albumin. — Slides 33–34 (Filtration Membranes)"},{"q":"The EPO-producing, oxygen-sensing (REPOS) cells of the kidney secrete which hormone in response to low oxygenation?","choices":["Renin secretion","Erythropoietin","Aldosterone","Calcitonin release"],"answer":1,"exp":"REPOS cells sense oxygenation levels and secrete erythropoietin, stimulating red blood cell production; juxtaglomerular (granular) cells instead secrete renin. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"}]};
